module bindings.ruby.http_message_body_framing_conflict;

// RFC 7230 gives an HTTP/1 message exactly one body length, and says what to do
// when the headers offer two. Section 3.3.2: a message with more than one
// Content-Length field "MUST either reject the message as invalid or replace
// the duplicated field-values with a single valid Content-Length field".
// Section 3.3.3: a message carrying both Transfer-Encoding and Content-Length
// "might indicate an attempt to perform request smuggling ... and ought to be
// handled as an error", and a sender forwarding it "MUST remove the received
// Content-Length field". A Ruby request reader that stores whichever framing
// value it sees and moves on is not wrong on its own bytes -- it is wrong
// relative to the next implementation on the connection, which may take the
// other reading, so one TCP stream becomes two different request sequences.
//
// The method this labels is the one where that obligation lands: the request
// reader that holds both framing values. In a Rack server the header store is
// the environment hash (the Rack spec documents CONTENT_LENGTH and the CGI
// HTTP_TRANSFER_ENCODING spelling), so the identification is a method that
// assigns both the transfer-encoding value and the content-length value out of
// that store, in a file that also asks the chunked question -- String#casecmp
// against the chunked constant, or a call into a chunked-body reader, the two
// spellings a Ruby parser uses for "is this the chunked framing". Puma's
// request reader spells it `te = @env[TRANSFER_ENCODING2]` falling through to
// `cl = @env[CONTENT_LENGTH]` whenever the encoding is neither chunked nor
// recognised, which silently takes the fixed-length reading of a message the
// front-end framed by the other header.
//
// The negation is the removal remedy RFC 7230 names, in the spellings a Ruby
// parser uses for it: deleting the conflicting framing key from the
// environment, or from a parsed header hash. It deliberately does not include
// the other remedy -- refusing the message -- because a raise statement in Ruby
// is reachable from this predicate only at file scope, and request readers
// commonly raise in sibling methods for unrelated malformed input (Puma's own
// reader raises ConnectionError and EOFError for socket conditions two methods
// away), so keying refusal would silence the finding on readers that never
// reconcile the two framings at all. A reader that remediates only by refusing
// the message is therefore still reported; the deletion is the one remedy whose
// spelling is not routine elsewhere in a request-parsing file.
//
// The two store reads are matched on assign tokens, which stay inside the
// method; the chunked question and the deletion are call tokens, which a Ruby
// file shares across its methods, so those two conjuncts hold at file scope: a
// file that asks the chunked question anywhere satisfies the identification,
// and a file that deletes the framing key anywhere is not reported. Recall
// residuals: an env value read without an assignment, a fetch-spelled
// env.fetch("HTTP_TRANSFER_ENCODING"), and a chunked question asked only as a
// bare `== "chunked"` comparison in a file whose calls name nothing chunked,
// all keep the weakness and are not reported.
//
// This is a property of the request reader rather than of any value flowing
// through it -- there is no untrusted argument to follow, because the
// attacker's input is the header block the method is reading -- so it is a
// presence fact on the method context, not a taint sink.

binding rubyHttpMessageBodyFramingConflict {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function"
    and node.context.language == "ruby"
    and containsAny(node.context.assign, ["env[TRANSFER_ENCODING", "env[HTTP_TRANSFER_ENCODING", "env['transfer-encoding'", "env[\"transfer-encoding\"", "env['HTTP_TRANSFER_ENCODING'", "env[\"HTTP_TRANSFER_ENCODING\""])
    and containsAny(node.context.assign, ["env[CONTENT_LENGTH", "env['CONTENT_LENGTH'", "env[\"CONTENT_LENGTH\"", "env['content-length'", "env[\"content-length\""])
    and containsAny(node.context.call, ["casecmp", "chunked"])
    and not (containsAny(node.context.callPath, ["env.delete", "headers.delete"]))
  emit issue code.AmbiguousMessageBodyFraming at node
}
